// The stop-the-world safepoint — `runtime/root/`'s `safepointEntry` +
// `safepointImpl` (runtime/root/:638-750) IN FULL: the mutator handshake (#1629) and
// the COLLECTOR half (#1633), so a Bit-only `libbitrt.a` actually collects.
//
// It was missed by every earlier audit because it is not an `export fn`: runtime/root/
// publishes it with `comptime { @export(&safepointEntry, .{ .name =
// "bit_rt_safepoint" }); }` (runtime/root/:753), which a `grep 'export fn bit_rt_'`
// enumeration cannot see. It is also the least optional symbol in the ABI — both
// backends SYNTHESIZE a call to it at every loop back-edge (compiler/x64value.bit,
// compiler/arm64compile.bit), so an image that does not define it does not link.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY IT IS ITS OWN MODULE AND NOT `runtime/gc`'s (#1633 moved it here)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #1629 landed it in `runtime/gc` because the mutator handshake is entirely the
// World registry's, which is `../gc/gcworld.bit`'s. The COLLECTOR half cannot
// live there, and the reason is structural rather than stylistic: a collection
// must enumerate root sources owned by `runtime/chan` (buffered `is_ref` channel
// elements, ABI.md §11) and by `runtime/sched` (every other task's stack and
// every task's `arg`, §5), and `runtime/gc` may not import either — `chan`
// imports `../gc`, so the reverse edge is an import cycle (E0044). `extern
// function` is not an escape: it is not on the `@nosplit` allowlist (§10.3) and
// a Linux `--emit-obj` rejects it outright (E0078, the wall SEAM 1 hit at #1600).
//
// So the composition moves to a LEAF module that may import all three, and
// nothing imports it. `../gc/gcworld.bit` keeps sole ownership of the World
// block address and the current-mutator cache — duplicating that cache would let
// one thread hold two slots, which the rendezvous then waits for forever — and
// publishes the two reads this file needs (`worldBlockAddr`, `worldEnsureSelf`).
//
// `runtime/stw` now holds two files (`stw.bit`, `stwpoll.bit`, split by
// #2478), so the #1511 sort-order rule is not satisfied by file count. It is
// satisfied for a different reason: a module's files are sorted bytewise
// (compiler/project.bit:7) but name resolution across them is
// order-independent — the checker types every top-level declaration before
// any function body (compiler/checkmodule.bit:255), so a reference to a
// sibling's const, type, function or method resolves identically whichever
// way the two files sort. File order controls only the order diagnostics are
// EMITTED, which is normative (spec/SPEC.md §14.8) but not a correctness
// concern here — and every helper either file calls is still a pinned symbol
// in another object regardless.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PIN CYCLE, AND WHY NOTHING HERE CAN CALL ITSELF (#1367)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is the pin-cycle trap (_tests_/bit/rootpins/) in its purest form: the
// backends emit a call to `bit_rt_safepoint` at every loop back edge, so a
// definition of `bit_rt_safepoint` that contains a loop back edge calls ITSELF.
// Before G2 (#1583) that call landed on a separate definition under the `_root`
// infix, so nothing — not a build, not a link, not a test run — could see the
// cycle. G2 left exactly one definition of the name, so there is no second one
// to absorb it: the same mistake is now an instant overflow in any loop.
//
// Two independent guarantees, both structural rather than asserted:
//
//   - `safepointEntry` is `@naked` and its body is a single `asm` block. §10.3.1
//     admits no locals, no control flow and no implicit prologue in a naked
//     function, and an `asm` payload is emitted verbatim, so there is no back
//     edge for codegen to attach a poll to. The emitted object carries no
//     relocation to any `bit_rt_` name (checked by `./make test-rootpins`, which
//     performs the G2 rename symbolically on the emitted object).
//   - EVERY OTHER function here is `@nosplit`, which suppresses back-edge poll
//     emission outright (§10.3), and whose default-deny allowlist additionally
//     refuses any call to a non-`@nosplit` function (E0075) — so the guarantee is
//     transitive over everything they reach, including the objects this file
//     drives, without anyone having to re-derive the call graph. That matters
//     most for the loops below: this file contains five, and each of them would
//     otherwise be a recursive poll.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ROOT SET, AND WHY IT IS ALL-OR-NOTHING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A collection that misses ONE root class does not crash — it sweeps a live
// object and corrupts the heap silently. So the five classes `runtime/root/`'s
// `scanRoots` (runtime/root/:406) funnels are enumerated here in the same order, and
// each names where it is implemented:
//
//   1. Buffered `is_ref` channel elements (§11) — `chanScanRegistryRoots`
//      (`../chan/chanreg.bit`, #1602). A buffered word is reachable only through
//      the ring while it sits unreceived. The ring is PROCESS memory, not a
//      GC-traced allocation, so its CONTENTS are the roots.
//   2. THIS thread's own frames, precisely (§4) — `gcScanSnapshot`
//      (`../gc/stackmap.bit`, #1633), walking the stack maps between
//      `stackMapsBegin()`/`stackMapsEnd()`.
//   3. Every OTHER stopped OS thread's frames, precisely — the same walk over
//      each parked mutator's published snapshot (`worldParkedFrame`). The world
//      is stopped, so each of those frames is valid and not moving.
//   4. Every OTHER task's stack, conservatively (§5) — claimed sound because a
//      parked task's callee-saved references are all spilled by the runtime
//      call chain that parked it. THAT CLAIM IS ONLY HALF TRUE HERE — see
//      class 7.
//   5. Every registered task's opaque `arg` (§5/§9) — a spawn thunk is a GC
//      object reachable ONLY through the pending task's `arg` until that task
//      runs and unpacks it.
//   6. Every registered task's per-task SCRATCH area (`stwScanScratch`, #1679).
//      THIS ONE HAS NO `runtime/root/` COUNTERPART. It exists because the port keeps
//      two live references (`scrElem`, the blocking channel value slot, and
//      `scrErr`, the pending-error object) in the `chanScratchWords` words at
//      the BASE of each task's stack, which is strictly BELOW `ctx.sp` and so
//      outside class 4's range for every task, always. A runtime that could use
//      thread-locals would keep the first as a stack local (class 4 covers it)
//      and the second in a thread-local.
//   7. Every OTHER task's own saved register file, conservatively
//      (`stwScanCtx`, #1742). THIS ONE ALSO HAS NO `runtime/root/` COUNTERPART, and
//      it is the fix to class 4's soundness gap: `runtime/root/`'s `park` hands off
//      through an ordinary LLVM call chain, whose callee-saved spills for a
//      still-live value land on a callee's STACK FRAME and so are already
//      inside class 4's `[sp, stackTop)` walk. This port's `schedSwitch`
//      (`../sched/sched.bit`) is hand-written `asm`, and it saves the
//      callee-saved file into the TASK'S OWN CONTROL BLOCK instead — a
//      separate allocation from its stack, outside class 4's range by
//      construction. A GC reference the register allocator keeps live in one
//      of those registers across a park (measured: a `string`'s pointer held
//      in a callee-saved register across a `sleep()` retry loop, #1742) was
//      invisible to every class above and collected while still needed —
//      silently, the way this header warns every missed class does. `runtime/root/`
//      never needed this class because it never had this problem.
//
//   8. The RUNNING task's own stack, conservatively (`stwScanTasks`, #1832).
//      THIS ONE HAS NO `runtime/root/` COUNTERPART EITHER, and like classes 6 and 7
//      it exists because of a property of the PORT rather than an oversight.
//
//      Class 2 covers this stack precisely, and precisely is not enough here. A
//      stack map names REFERENCE-TYPED slots; an `int` slot that happens to hold
//      an object address is invisible to it by construction. The runtime is full
//      of exactly that: the ABI boundary FORCES a pinned symbol to take its
//      argument as a bare `int` (E0079 — a pinned symbol may not name a managed
//      type), so `bit_rt_chan_send` receives the element it is about to store as
//      an `int`, and holds it as one across its own loop.
//
//      And a collection CAN land there. Codegen synthesizes `bl
//      bit_rt_safepoint` at every loop back-edge, so any `bit_rt_*` implemented
//      in Bit and NOT marked `@nosplit` can be interrupted mid-operation
//      (#1656/#1658). Six such pins are on the channel path alone —
//      `bit_rt_chan_send/recv/make/close`, `bit_rt_select`,
//      `bit_rt_select_alloc`. The pre-port runtime was structurally immune: it
//      carried no back-edge polls, so no collection could occur inside a channel
//      operation there at all.
//
//      So the running task's stack needs the same conservative treatment class 4
//      gives every other task's. The low bound is the LIVE `sp`, not the
//      snapshot's `fp`: frame pointers grow toward higher addresses, so
//      `[fp, top)` is the CALLER frames and excludes the innermost frame's own
//      locals — which is precisely where the raw `int` sits. That distinction is
//      MEASURED, not argued (`_tests_/stress/quicwire` under `BIT_GC=stress`,
//      12 packets over channels with `select`): bad-packet rate 2 of 8 with no
//      class 8, 1 of 12 bounded at `fp`, 0 of 14 bounded at `sp`.
//
//      IT IS NOT FREE, unlike every class above it. Under `BIT_GC=stress` —
//      a collection at every safepoint — quicwire goes from ~50s to ~157s,
//      because this walks the live stack on every collection instead of only
//      the frames a stack map names. That is a test policy, not production, but
//      the cost is real and belongs in the record rather than in a surprise.
//
//   9. A thread parked in a `syscall` region, conservatively — the lowest
//      stack pointer it has published as parked there (`stwSyscallLo`), plus
//      its saved callee-saved register file (`stwScanSyscallRegs`), both
//      #1904. Neither `blocked` (holds no live references, by contract) nor
//      `running` (waited for) fits a thread genuinely stopped in the kernel
//      while still holding references — `bit_rt_os_run`'s `waitpid` blocks
//      for a subprocess's whole lifetime holding its argv, path and
//      output-buffer references (`runtime/ABI.md` §5, "The `syscall`
//      contract"), so it must be SCANNED where it stands. The register half
//      closes the same #1742 gap class 7 closes, over `mSyscall` instead of
//      `mParked`.
//
//   10. Every OTHER stopped mutator's own stack, conservatively
//      (`stwParkedLo`, #1834). Class 8 is only half the answer it reads as: it
//      lowers the bound for the ONE stack the collector is standing on, found
//      by asking which task's mapping contains the LIVE `sp`. There is only one
//      live `sp` and it is the collector's, so every other thread that is
//      stopped at a safepoint keeps class 4's `ctxSp` bound — where that task
//      last PARKED, which is above the frames it is actually sitting in, so
//      stacks grow down and the walk starts past everything it holds.
//
//      What is left covering it is class 3's PRECISE walk, and precisely is not
//      enough for exactly the reason class 8 exists: a stack map names
//      reference-typed slots, and `bit_rt_chan_send` holds its element as a
//      bare `int`. Two threads in `bit_rt_chan_send` at once is therefore one
//      scanned stack and one unscanned one.
//
//      NO ABI CHANGE WAS NEEDED, and the shape of that is the whole class. A
//      parked mutator publishes `muFrame` — the address of the `SafepointFrame`
//      the `@naked` shim reserved BELOW its own entry `sp` on that thread's own
//      stack. The frame's ADDRESS is therefore already a valid, and strictly
//      lower, stack pointer for that thread; only the frame's CONTENTS
//      (`ret`/`fp`/`regs`) were ever being read. #1834 proposed adding an `sp`
//      field to §4 for this and it is unnecessary — the published address is
//      the better bound, because it also brings the frame's own `regs[32]`
//      inside the conservative range, which closes #1742's raw-int-in-a-
//      callee-saved-register case for stopped mutators as well.
//
//      This is class 9's shape over a different state: a thread that cannot
//      answer for itself has its bound published on its behalf, and the bound
//      is only ever LOWERED, which class 4's header already argues is safe.
//
// CLASS 8b (#2650) — the classes above cover a stack only when some REGISTERED
// TASK's mapping contains it, because that mapping is the only source of the
// stack's TOP. A collection taken on a thread's own OS-thread stack (a worker
// between tasks, the bootstrap thread before the first task) belongs to no
// task, so classes 8 and 10 both find nothing to lower and no range to scan.
// Measured on this tree: 1353-1771 of ~40000 collections under
// `BIT_GC=stress`, 0 of 190 under the default policy (`stwUnheldCount` below)
// — the SAME population `stwScanTasks` counts as `held == false`.
//
// `stwScanOwnStack` (below `stwScanTasks`) closes this the same way class 8
// closes it for a task-held stack, keyed off `slot`'s owning OS thread's own
// stack TOP and BASE (`worldStackTop`/`worldStackBase`, `../gc/gcworld.bit`,
// #2646/#3516), both published once by that thread itself, 0 when unknown.
// Called from `stwScanTasks` after the registry walk, only when `held`
// stayed false, with the collector's own slot threaded down from
// `stwCollect`'s `selfSlot`; it scans `[sp, top)` exactly as
// `stwScanStackRange` does for every other class, and refuses — scans
// nothing, touches nothing — unless `sp` is actually CONTAINED between
// `base` and `top`, for the same reason class 8's header gives: past the
// real stack top is unmapped, and a faulting collector is worse than the
// sweep it prevents.
//
// POPULATED IN PRODUCTION SINCE #2649/03d15de3, AND THAT EXPOSED A REAL BUG
// (#3514/#3516). #2646 added `worldStackTop`'s storage and doors with no
// caller, so `top` read 0 for every slot and this class was correctly wired
// but a permanent no-op — the measured population above was still actually
// uncovered on a live runtime. #2649 gave it its only caller
// (`../gc/gcworldsync.bit`'s `mutatorSlot`, on the claim), which made this
// class live for the first time — but its guard back then was only `top <=
// 0 || top <= sp`, an upper bound with no lower one. `sp` is the COLLECTING
// thread's live stack pointer, which is not always on that thread's OWN
// OS-thread stack — it may be on a runtime-allocated TASK stack instead
// (`_tests_/stress/stwcollect`'s private-world design puts it there on
// purpose) — and on macOS a task stack sits at a LOWER address than the
// pthread stack, so `top <= sp` never caught the mismatch: the collector
// conservatively scanned from the task stack's `sp` up to the OS thread's
// `top`, across ~1.86 GB of unrelated mappings, and faulted. #3516 adds
// `worldStackBase` alongside `worldStackTop`, published in the same call, so
// the guard can require actual containment instead of an upper bound alone.
// #1837 tracks the remaining precision gap (deriving `sp`'s frame from the
// frame-pointer chain instead needs the stack-map validation in
// `../gc/stackmap.bit`, which the explicit registry fields avoid).
//
// Classes 4, 5, 7, 8 and 10 are reachable only when a scheduler block is bound;
// class 1 only when a channel registry is. `stwBind` takes all three addresses TOGETHER
// and `stwCanCollect` refuses to collect unless every one of them is present —
// deliberately, because "collect with a smaller root set" is the failure mode
// this whole comment exists to prevent. An unbound runtime does not collect; it
// never collects PARTIALLY.

import { worldPark, worldRendezvous, worldRestart } from "../gc"
import { worldLockTry, worldLockRelease, worldParkedFrame } from "../gc"
import { worldStackTop, worldStackBase } from "../gc"
import { worldSyscallSp, worldSyscallRegs, worldSyscallRegWords, worldHighWaterMark } from "../gc"
import { worldEnsureSelfOn } from "../gc"
import { maxMutators } from "../gc"
import { gcShouldCollect, gcMarkBegin, gcCollectFinish } from "../gc"
import { gcMarkConservative, gcScanSnapshot } from "../gc"
import { wordAt, loadWord } from "../gc"
// stwPoll's hot path inlines worldStopRequested/gcShouldCollect's bodies
// directly (#1700, avoids a cross-module call); these offsets are what it
// needs to do that. MUST STAY IN LOCKSTEP with the real definitions in
// ../gc/gcworldstop.bit, ../gc/gc.bit and ../alloc/alloc.bit — a drifted copy
// here silently disables collection.
import { worldStopWord } from "../gc"
// gcNumObjectsIdx/gcNextObjects (#3292): the object half of the trigger,
// mirrored here for the same reason the byte half already is. `gcAddrMax` was
// imported alongside them and is gone again (#3359): the second-growth-cycle
// ceiling is a subtraction now, so nothing here needs the saturation bound.
import { gcHeapAddr, gcCfgEnabled, gcCfgStress, gcNextBytes, gcNumObjectsIdx, gcNextObjects } from "../gc"
import { heapLiveWord } from "../alloc"
import { chanScanRegistryRoots } from "../chan"
import { taskAt, schedMaxRegistered } from "../sched"
import { schedRegLock, schedRegUnlock, schedRegHead, schedRegNext } from "../sched"
import { ctxSp, ctxWords, taskArg, taskState, taskDone, taskStackBase, taskStackBytes } from "../sched"
// The per-task scratch area's word count (#1679) — see `stwScanScratch`.
import { chanScratchWords, scrCounter } from "../sched"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The bound blocks
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// CALLER-OWNED, the convention the remaining ported runtime blocks follow
// (`chanBootBind`): the owner (`runtime/root`, which already holds `gcState`)
// passes the addresses in once at boot. Zero until then, and zero means "do not
// collect" rather than "collect with less".
//
// The World block is NO LONGER one of them (#2184). It was, and `worldBind` was
// the door; `runtime/gc` owns it outright now, because a door taking an address
// can be handed collectable memory and that was #1991. The convention survived
// here only because these three blocks genuinely live in another module.

let gcAddr: i64 = 0
let schedAddr: i64 = 0
let chanRegAddr: i64 = 0
// Cached alongside the three above (#1700): stwPoll's hot path reads this
// directly instead of calling `worldBlockAddr`, avoiding a cross-module call.
let worldAddr: i64 = 0

// Collections given up because the stop-the-world rendezvous expired —
// `runtime/gc/`'s `stats.stw_abandoned`, kept here rather than in the collector block
// because that block is full (`gcWords` = 39, every word assigned) and its size
// is `runtime/root`'s declaration. Abandoning is SAFE (skipping a collection
// only costs heap growth) but is evidence that some thread blocks without the
// `blocked` contract, so it is counted and readable.
let stwAbandoned: i64 = 0

// Statically bounded conservative stack scan (Power of 10): 32MB of words, far
// above the 64KB task stacks the scheduler maps.
const stwMaxStackWords = 4194304

// Bind the three process-wide blocks a collection needs. Called once at boot by
// `runtime/root`, after it has `ptrOf`'d its own collector state, scheduler and
// channel registry (which this `@nosplit` module cannot do for it). The World
// World block is not passed at all — `../gc` owns it (#2184); `world` below is
// its address read back through `rootWorldAddr`, cached for the hot path (#1700).
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_bind") fn stwBind(
  g: int,
  sched: int,
  chanReg: int,
  world: int,
) {
  gcAddr = g
  schedAddr = sched
  chanRegAddr = chanReg
  worldAddr = world
}

// Abandoned-collection count, so a test can assert it stayed 0.
//
// ATOMIC (#3299): `stwAbandoned` is written from `stwPollOn`'s locked section
// (`stwpoll.bit`), but that lock is keyed off the CALLER-SUPPLIED world
// address, not a single fixed cell — `stwPollOn` is also driven directly, with
// a private world/gc/sched block, by `_tests_/stress/stwcollect` (see its
// comment on `stwPoll`, above). Two independent lock domains do not exclude
// each other, and #1833 already recorded a live worker joining a test's own
// rendezvous, so a live collection and a test-driven one CAN be inside their
// respective locked sections at the same real time while both write this one
// shared module total. Plain load/add is a real lost-increment hazard here.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_abandoned") fn stwAbandonedCount(): int {
  let n = atomicLoad(ptrOf(stwAbandoned))
  return n
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The running-stack coverage counters (#1837)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `stwScans` counts root enumerations; `stwUnheld` counts the subset in which NO
// registered task's mapping contained the collecting thread's live `sp`;
// `stwOwnScans` counts the subset of THOSE in which `stwScanOwnStack` (#2650)
// actually scanned a range, rather than refusing on an unpublished or
// non-containing bound.
//
// COUNTED RATHER THAN ASSERTED, and PERMANENT rather than re-derived. The
// difference is not a bug by itself — it is the population in which classes 8
// and 10 cannot fire, and a class that silently does not fire is exactly the
// shape this file's ROOT SET header warns about: no build, link, golden or
// stress gate can tell "the running stack was covered" from "the scan was inert
// on every collection". #1837 was measured by adding these words and then
// REVERTING them, which is why its own repro field has to describe how to add
// them back; keeping them makes the number one build away instead of one
// re-instrumentation away, and makes any fix for #1837 falsifiable by the same
// reading. Measured here: 0 of 190 under the default policy, 1353-1771 of
// ~40000 under `BIT_GC=stress`.
//
// EVERY `stwUnheld` INCREMENT ALSO RUNS CLASS 8b (#2650): the increment above
// and the call to `stwScanOwnStack` sit in the same `if (!held)` guard in
// `stwScanTasks`, so `stwUnheld` and the number of ATTEMPTS at class 8b can
// never drift apart by construction — there is no separate "did class 8b get
// a chance to run" question to ask. `stwOwnScans` is the narrower question:
// whether that attempt actually scanned anything. #2649/#3516 (landed,
// `03d15de3`) publish `worldStackTop`/`worldStackBase` on the claim before
// `stwScanTasks` can ever reach a collection on that thread (`stwPollOn`
// calls `worldEnsureSelfOn` before anything else), so on a live boot
// `stwOwnScanCount() == stwUnheldCount()` is the expected steady-state
// reading rather than a permanent 0 — a gap between them means some unheld
// collection reached class 8b's guard and refused, which
// `_tests_/stress/stwownstack` (#2651) asserts against directly.
let stwScans: i64 = 0
let stwUnheld: i64 = 0
let stwOwnScans: i64 = 0

// Root enumerations performed by this collector.
//
// ATOMIC (#3299): same reason as `stwAbandonedCount` above — `stwScanTasks`
// (below) writes this from inside `stwPollOn`'s locked section, and that lock
// does not exclude a second caller driving a private world (`_tests_/stress/stwcollect`).
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_scans") fn stwScanCount(): int {
  let n = atomicLoad(ptrOf(stwScans))
  return n
}

// The subset of those in which the collecting thread was standing on a stack no
// registered task owns — a worker's or the bootstrap thread's OWN OS-thread
// stack.
//
// ATOMIC (#3299): same reason as `stwScanCount` above.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_unheld") fn stwUnheldCount(): int {
  let n = atomicLoad(ptrOf(stwUnheld))
  return n
}

// The subset of `stwUnheld` in which `stwScanOwnStack` actually scanned a
// range — `worldStackTop`/`worldStackBase` were both published for this slot
// and actually contained `sp` (#2650). Should read equal to
// `stwUnheldCount()` on a live boot post-#2649/#3516; see the header comment
// above `stwScans` for why a gap between the two is the signal to chase.
//
// ATOMIC (#3299): same reason as `stwScanCount` above.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_own_scans") fn stwOwnScanCount(): int {
  let n = atomicLoad(ptrOf(stwOwnScans))
  return n
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Root enumeration moved to `stwscan.bit` (#4322)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `stwScanStackRange` .. `stwScanTasks` — the ten root-enumeration helpers
// `stwCollect` below calls — were moved verbatim into the sibling
// `stwscan.bit`. This file was 796 lines against the hard 800-line E0200
// ceiling and the next one-line edit would have broken the gate. Same module,
// same imports, no resolution change.

// One full stop-the-world mark-sweep. The world is already stopped and this
// thread holds the world lock; `snap` is this thread's own `SafepointFrame`.
//
// `gcMarkBegin` .. `gcCollectFinish` is `runtime/gc/`'s `collect` with the root
// callback inverted (`../gc/gccollect.bit` deviation 2) — everything between the
// two is the root enumeration, in `runtime/root/`'s `scanRoots` order.
@nosplit fn stwCollect(g: *i64, world: *i64, selfSlot: int, snap: int, sched: int, chanReg: int) {
  gcMarkBegin(g)

  // 1. Buffered `is_ref` channel elements (§11).
  chanScanRegistryRoots(g, wordAt(chanReg))

  // 2. This thread's own frames, precisely (§4).
  gcScanSnapshot(g, snap)

  // 3. Every other stopped OS thread's frames, precisely.
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: exactly `maxMutators` slots.
  while (i < maxMutators) {
    if (i != selfSlot) {
      let f = worldParkedFrame(world, i)
      if (f != 0) {
        gcScanSnapshot(g, f)
      }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }

  // 4 + 5 + 9. Every other task's stack, conservatively, and every task's `arg`
  // — plus, for a task a syscall-parked thread is standing in, the bound and
  // register file that thread published (#1904). Also covers class 8b
  // (#2650) when none of them holds this thread's own live `sp`.
  stwScanTasks(g, world, taskAt(sched), selfSlot)

  gcCollectFinish(g)
}

